  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)^{2}(1-x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ where\n\n- $x=-2$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=1$ is a simple root.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a negative leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/9380e0f00b0df89a4148aa184ad73a1edfad8b76c9755f23926d6b856fdddb62.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)<0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is below the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
